From: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Subject: kernfs: path lookup scalability improvements
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:00:14 -0800

Patch series "kernfs: lockless path lookup and stat()".

A path lookup in sysfs or cgroupfs leaves RCU walk at its first kernfs
component and then takes kernfs_iattr_rwsem for reading in every directory
of the path.  There is one of those rwsems per filesystem.  stat() takes
it once more in ->getattr().

That costs scalability: however many CPUs are doing lookups, each of these
filesystems manages about 1.5 million per second in total.  And it costs
reliability: one reader which is preempted while it holds the rwsem, plus
one chown() waiting for it, stalls every other lookup and stat() in the
filesystem.

Patch 1 keeps lookups of cached paths in RCU walk, with no kernfs lock.
Patch 2 makes ->getattr() lockless in the same way.

In a 64-CPU guest with 64 processes, each using a different cgroup file,
patch 1 made access() 49x faster, open() and close() 22x faster and stat()
13x faster.  Patch 2 made stat() a further 3x faster.  A process running
alone gained 23% to 46% from patch 1 and nothing from patch 2.

A single process reading the files of 500 cgroups, while a chown() waited
behind a preempted reader, had sweeps of 1.2 to 1.8 seconds where 17ms is
normal.  With patch 1 its longest sweep was 29ms if it only opened the
files, and with patch 2 also if it called stat() on each first.

Not covered: a lookup through a kernfs symlink (everything under
/sys/class) still leaves RCU walk, as do lookups of names which are not
in the dcache.  readdir is untouched.  Many processes opening the same
file are limited by a per-file mutex and gained nothing.

The permission and stat() results were checked while the owner of a
directory was being changed, more than 100 million times each, with no
wrong result, and each check has a negative control which fails.  Lockdep,
KASAN and PREEMPT_RT kernels reported nothing.

This is on top of Shakeel Butt's "kernfs: stop taking kernfs_iattr_rwsem
for writing in mkdir/rmdir" [1].


This patch (of 2):

Improve kernfs scalability by keeping path lookups in RCU walk, which
takes kernfs_iattr_rwsem and the dentry reference counts out of them.
Throughput improvements of more than 40x were measured for highly
contended workloads in a 64-CPU guest.

The main work happens in kernfs_iop_permission() and
kernfs_dop_revalidate().  The latter simply stops refusing RCU walk.  In
the former the inode's mode/uid/gid are checked against the kernfs_node's
under a new per-fs seqcount.  If they match then the inode needs no
refresh and we avoid taking kernfs_iattr_rwsem.  So

        seq = raw_read_seqcount(&root->iattr_seq);
        if (seq is odd)
                slowpath;       /* a setattr is in progress */
        if (inode's mode/uid/gid != kernfs_node's mode/uid/gid)
                slowpath;
        ret = generic_permission();
        if (read_seqcount_retry(&root->iattr_seq, seq))
                slowpath;
        return ret;

where the slowpath leaves RCU walk, takes kernfs_iattr_rwsem and refreshes
the inode, as before.

The tricky part is kernfs_iop_permission()'s call of
kernfs_permission_lockless().  This lockless comparison is safe due to
three observed behaviors.  These behaviors introduce some fragility - they
must be preserved.

1: Every change to a visible node's mode or owner goes through
   __kernfs_setattr() with kernfs_iattr_rwsem held for writing, inside a
   write section of the seqcount.  The only other store to the inode's
   copies is kernfs_refresh_inode() copying from the node under the read
   lock.

   Comments were added to these sites to explain the dependency.

2: The node, its iattrs and the root are all freed through RCU.

   Comments were added to these sites to explain the dependency.

3: generic_permission() consults only the mode, uid and gid of the
   inode.  True for kernfs because it has no POSIX ACLs.

   This is an expansion of generic_permission()'s contract.  A comment
   was added to generic_permission() to explain this, with a reference
   to kernfs.

Detailed performance and correctness testing results are presented below
this patch's "---" so they can be viewed via this patch's Link:.

